# Anatomy

- **Embryological development of the stomach** - derived from the tubular embryonic <u>foregut</u>:
  - <u>5th week of gestation</u> - dilatation of the caudal portion and subsequent being invested by two mesenteries:
    - **Dorsal mesentery** - subsequently becomes the gastrophrenic, gastrosplenic, and gastrocolic ligaments
    - **Ventral mesentry** - subsequently because the hepatoduodenal, gastrohepatic ligaments of the <u>lesser omentum</u> and the falciform ligament
  - <u>7th week of gestation</u> - stomach descends, rotates, and dilates w/ disproportionate elongation of the greater curvature into its normal anatomical shape and position
- **Anatomical division of the stomach and anatomical sites of the stomach** - anatomically fixed proximally as OGJ and didstally at pylorus, but the midportion is mobile:
  - **Divisions:**
    - <u>Cardia</u> - most proximal region of the stomach and attaches to the esophagus, just about it is the physiologically competent lower esophageal sphincter (LES)
    - <u>Fundus</u> - superior-most region of the stomach and is floppy and distensible
    - <u>Body</u> (corpus) - largest portion of the stomach bounded left by the relatively straight **lesser curvature**, and on the right by the longer **greater curvature**
    - <u>Antrum</u> - horizontal region of the stomach that leads to the duodenum through the pylorus
  - **Anatomical sites:**
    - <u>Angle of His</u> - important anatomical angle formed by the fungus and the left margin of the esophagus, contributing to the **anti-reflux barrier**
    - <u>OGJ</u> - junction between esophagus and the stomach, as defined arbitrarily but the most proximal site of the gastric rugae, approximately 2-3cm below the diaphragmatic esophageal hiatus (at 7th costosternal articulation)

  
  ![](img/sabiston_divisions_of_the_stomach.png)
- **Blood supply of the stomach:**
  - **Arterial supply of the stomach** - from the <u>celiac artery</u>:
    - <u>Right gastric artery</u> - branch of **hepatic artery** and runs along the lesser curvature (anastamosed w/ left gastric artery)
    - <u>Left gastric artery</u> - branch of **celiac trunk** and runs along the lesser curvature (N.B. 15-20% popul)
    - <u>Right gastroepiploic artery</u> - branch of **gastroduodenal artery** and runs along the greater curvature
    - <u>Left gastroepiploic artery</u> - branch of **splenic artery** and runs along greater curvature
    - <u>Short gastric arteries</u> - direct branches of **splenic artery** supplying the proximal stomach w/ contributions by inferior phrenic artery
  - **Venous drainage of the stomach** - mirrors arterial supply:
    - <u>Right gastric vein</u> - drains into portal vein
    - <u>Left gastric vein</u> - drains into portal vein
    - <u>Right gastroepiploic vein</u> - drains into superior mesenteric vein
    - <u>Left gastroepiploic vein</u> - drains into splenic vein

  
  ![](img/blood_supply_of_stomach_sabiston.png)
- **Lymphatic drainage of the stomach** - all eventually drain into celiac nodes and thoracic duct:
  - <u>Superior gastric group</u> - drains into left gastric and paracardial nodes
  - <u>Suprapyloric group</u> - drains into right suprapancreatic nodes
  - <u>Pancreaticolienal group</u> - drains into left gastroepiploic and splenic nodes
  - <u>Subpyloric group</u> - drains into rightepiploic nodes

  
  ![](img/sabiston_lymphatic_drainage_of_stomach.png)

