# Gastric Cancer

- **Pathology** - different classification schemes have been proposed:
  - **Lauren classification** (1965) - intestinal vs diffuse subtypes:
    - **Intestinal subtype** - well-differentiated adenocarcinoma w/ glandular formation occuring in areas of pre-malignant conditions, e.g. gastric atrophy, intestinal metaplasia (e.g. environmental exposure)
    - **Diffuse type** - poorly-differented w/ small clusters of signet ring cells spreading submucosally (linitis plastica)
- **Dx of gastric cancer** - histological diagnosis after OGD and Bx
- **Staging System** - 8th edition AJCC TNM classification: 
![](img/tnm_staging_stomach_sabiston.png)
- **Principles of pre-operative staging workup** - assess extent of disease, prognosis and Mx options:
  - **Treatment modalities:**
    - <u>Endoscopic Tx</u> - for early gastric cancer
    - <u>Uprfont surgery +/- adjuvant therapy</u>
    - <u>Neoadjuvant chemotherapy followed by resection</u>
    - <u>Systemic therapy w/o resection</u> - metastatic disease w/o obstruction
- **Staging Ix** - endoscopy and EUS, cross-sectional imaging (contrast CT, MRI, PET-CT), diagnostic paparoscopy: 
![](img/general_staging_and_tx_for_gastric_cancer.png)
  - **Upper Endoscopy and Endoscopic Ultrasound**:
    - <u>Role of endoscopy</u>:
      - Biopsy of lesion - to obtain correct histological diagnosis of gastric adenocarcinoma
      - Endoscopy lesion (\<= 2cm diameter lesions) - endoscopic mucosal resection (EMR) or endoscopic submucosal dissection (ESD) is potentially curative for early gastric cancer
    - <u>Role of endoscopic USG</u> - locoregional staging if no evidence of metastatic disease (i.e. after CT):
      - 1\. **T-staging** - assess depth of of tumour invasion:
        - Mucosa and submucosa - constitutes first 3 layers (T1 tumour)
        - Muscularis propria - constitutes 4th layer (T2 tumour if within MP, T3 if beyond MP)
        - Serosa - constitutes bright 5th layer (T4 tumour if serosa signal lost)
      - 2\. **N-staging** - asssessment of all perigastric LN involvement +/- FNA through EUS guidance
      - 3\. **Assessment of surounding organ infiltration** - e.g. spleen or pancreas
      - 4\. **Pick up small amounts of ascites**

      
      ![](img/eus_gastric_cancer.png)
    - <u>Differentiation between high-risk and low-risk disease</u> - prognostic and informs potential resectability:
      - Low risk disease - T1/2, N0 disease
      - High risk disease - T3/4 or \>N0
    - <u>Test properties of EUS</u> - operator dependent:
      - **T-staging** (differentiating between T1/T2 and T3/4 disease) - 86% Sn, 90% Sp
      - **N-staging** - 83% Sn, 67% Sp
  - **Contrast CT T+A+P** - evaluation of metastatic disease (often 1st Ix as to consider whether to proceed w/ staging Ix)
  - **PET-CT** - role in locally-advanced cancer or those considering neo-adjuvant therapy to detect occult metastasis (NCCN guidelines recommend PET-CT as part of staging for \>T1 disease w/o evidence of metastatic disease on initial CT)
  - **Staging laparoscopy** - detect occult peritoneal metastasis for \>T2 disease
- **Mx:**
  - **Potential Tx modalities:**
    - <u>Isolated Endoscopic Tx</u> - e.g. EMR, ESD
    - <u>Upfront surgery +/- adjuvant therapy</u> - complete resection (i.e. R0 resection with D2 dissection)
    - <u>Neoadjuvant chemotherapy followed by resection</u>
    - <u>Systemic therapy w/o resection</u>
  - **Surgical Tx:**
    - **Complete gastric resection of gastric tumour w/ wide resection margins** - standard of care for resectable cancer:
      - **Approaches** - open vs minimally invasive (e.g. endoscopic resection or laparoscopy)
      - **Unresectable gastric cancer:**
        - 1\. Metastatic disease - e.g. liver, or peritoneal metastasis
        - 2\. Invasion into unresectable vascular structures - e.g. aorta, celiac trunk,

